= Emergence =

== Overview ==
'''Emergence''' describes higher-level patterns, properties, or behaviors that arise from interactions among lower-level components and are not simply properties of isolated components.

The term is used in complex systems, physics, biology, philosophy, cognitive science, and social science.

== Levels of description ==
An emergent property is attributed at one scale or organizational level even though it depends on lower-level components.

Examples can include collective motion, phase behavior, traffic waves, flock structure, market patterns, or network-level synchronization.

== Weak and strong senses ==
Different literatures use “emergence” differently.

A weak sense emphasizes collective behavior that can in principle be explained or simulated from lower-level rules.

Stronger philosophical senses make additional claims about irreducibility, novelty, or causal autonomy.

These should not be silently conflated.

== Emergence and unpredictability ==
An emergent pattern can be surprising or difficult to predict, but:

<pre>
emergence ≠ unpredictability by definition
</pre>

A pattern can be emergent even if theory eventually explains it.

== Emergence and self-organization ==
[[Self-organization]] concerns a process by which organized pattern forms through decentralized interactions.

Emergence concerns the higher-level pattern/property itself.

The concepts often overlap but are not synonyms.

== Related concepts ==
[[Complex system]], [[Self-organization]], [[Feedback]], [[Network]], [[System boundary]], and [[Distributed cognition]] provide the multiscale context.

